1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,319
Senior Angular interview preparation, lesson 2.

2
00:00:03,319 --> 00:00:07,192
Design Tokens: Themes, Accessibility, and Public Contracts.

3
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:11,942
A design system is a contract between teams

4
00:00:13,067 --> 00:00:14,973
Welcome to the design tokens lesson.

5
00:00:14,973 --> 00:00:18,150
We will review four questions: raw values and token layers,

6
00:00:18,150 --> 00:00:21,009
renaming a public token, dark mode and forced colors,

7
00:00:21,009 --> 00:00:23,233
and controls that break when text grows.

8
00:00:23,233 --> 00:00:27,046
Before looking at those defects, we need to understand what tokens promise.

9
00:00:27,046 --> 00:00:29,587
A token is a named design decision that

10
00:00:29,587 --> 00:00:32,129
can be reused by code and design tools.

11
00:00:32,129 --> 00:00:36,577
Its value might be a color, a distance, a font size, or a duration.

12
00:00:36,577 --> 00:00:41,025
A useful name explains the role of that decision rather than its current appearance.

13
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:45,641
A design system is a contract between teams · 2

14
00:00:46,767 --> 00:00:50,008
The consumer of a shared library should be able to

15
00:00:50,008 --> 00:00:52,925
change a supported theme without copying a component's stylesheet.

16
00:00:52,925 --> 00:00:56,166
If every component chooses its own blue, radius, and spacing,

17
00:00:56,166 --> 00:00:59,083
a brand refresh becomes a search through unrelated code.

18
00:00:59,083 --> 00:01:03,297
More subtly, a dark theme can change a background without changing its foreground.

19
00:01:03,297 --> 00:01:07,511
The result can be technically consistent with the old stylesheet and still unreadable.

20
00:01:07,511 --> 00:01:11,725
Tokens let us express related decisions together and review them as a system.

21
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:16,649
A design system is a contract between teams · 3

22
00:01:17,775 --> 00:01:22,168
In an interview, do not stop at replacing hexadecimal values with variables.

23
00:01:22,168 --> 00:01:25,096
Explain the source of truth, the token layers,

24
00:01:25,096 --> 00:01:28,391
the override boundary, and how you test the result.

25
00:01:28,391 --> 00:01:30,954
A senior answer also considers existing consumers.

26
00:01:30,954 --> 00:01:36,079
A public CSS custom property is an interface even though TypeScript cannot see it.

27
00:01:36,079 --> 00:01:41,205
Its name, meaning, inheritance, and fallback behavior can all become dependencies for another team.

28
00:01:42,580 --> 00:01:44,533
Primitive, semantic, and component layers

29
00:01:45,658 --> 00:01:48,783
Primitive tokens describe a palette or scale: a

30
00:01:48,783 --> 00:01:51,908
blue step, a spacing increment, or a radius.

31
00:01:51,908 --> 00:01:53,471
They are reusable ingredients.

32
00:01:53,471 --> 00:01:55,815
Semantic tokens describe intent: action background,

33
00:01:55,815 --> 00:01:58,159
content on an action, surface border,

34
00:01:58,159 --> 00:01:59,331
or disabled foreground.

35
00:01:59,331 --> 00:02:03,238
Components consume semantic roles so they do not need to

36
00:02:03,238 --> 00:02:07,145
know which palette step implements a role in each theme.

37
00:02:08,520 --> 00:02:11,250
Primitive, semantic, and component layers · 2

38
00:02:12,375 --> 00:02:16,665
A component token is justified when consumers need a documented component-specific

39
00:02:16,665 --> 00:02:20,954
override that should not affect every use of the semantic role.

40
00:02:20,954 --> 00:02:24,464
A button's corner shape might be one such decision.

41
00:02:24,464 --> 00:02:27,974
Creating a token for every declaration is usually counterproductive.

42
00:02:27,974 --> 00:02:31,484
It exposes implementation details and makes future refactoring expensive.

43
00:02:31,484 --> 00:02:34,604
Start with reusable semantic decisions, then add component

44
00:02:34,604 --> 00:02:37,724
tokens when a real supported customization requires them.

45
00:02:39,100 --> 00:02:41,559
Primitive, semantic, and component layers · 3

46
00:02:42,684 --> 00:02:45,846
The example separates a palette value from its purpose.

47
00:02:45,846 --> 00:02:49,359
In dark mode, the semantic role can point to another

48
00:02:49,359 --> 00:02:52,522
primitive while the component still consumes the same name.

49
00:02:52,522 --> 00:02:55,332
Define foreground and background roles as a pair

50
00:02:55,332 --> 00:02:58,143
and verify their contrast for every relevant state.

51
00:02:58,143 --> 00:03:02,360
A hover role, selected role, and focus indicator need deliberate design; deriving

52
00:03:02,360 --> 00:03:06,225
all of them with opacity can fail against a different background.

53
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:08,980
Inheritance and override placement

54
00:03:10,105 --> 00:03:13,689
CSS custom properties participate in the cascade and ordinarily inherit.

55
00:03:13,689 --> 00:03:16,915
A product can set a token on a theme

56
00:03:16,915 --> 00:03:19,782
container and descendants can consume the inherited value.

57
00:03:19,782 --> 00:03:24,083
This is an important part of a scoped theme: two regions on

58
00:03:24,083 --> 00:03:28,385
one page can use different semantic mappings without a global JavaScript toggle.

59
00:03:29,760 --> 00:03:31,752
Inheritance and override placement · 2

60
00:03:32,877 --> 00:03:34,870
Be careful where you declare defaults.

61
00:03:34,870 --> 00:03:38,524
If a component declares its public override property directly on its

62
00:03:38,524 --> 00:03:42,177
host, that declaration wins over an inherited value on an ancestor.

63
00:03:42,177 --> 00:03:46,827
The consumer may have set the documented property correctly and still see no change.

64
00:03:46,827 --> 00:03:51,145
Prefer consuming a public property with a fallback to the library's semantic role.

65
00:03:51,145 --> 00:03:55,131
The fallback supplies a default when the consumer did not provide a

66
00:03:55,131 --> 00:03:58,784
value; it does not need to overwrite the consumer's inherited decision.

67
00:04:00,160 --> 00:04:02,373
Inheritance and override placement · 3

68
00:04:03,498 --> 00:04:06,078
Debug this in the browser's computed styles.

69
00:04:06,078 --> 00:04:09,396
Check the property on the theme ancestor and on

70
00:04:09,396 --> 00:04:12,714
the component host, then inspect the actual background declaration.

71
00:04:12,714 --> 00:04:16,401
A variable's existence does not prove the component reads it.

72
00:04:16,401 --> 00:04:21,194
Also remember that a fallback handles a missing variable; a present value that

73
00:04:21,194 --> 00:04:25,986
is invalid for the consuming property can invalidate that declaration at computed-value time.

74
00:04:25,986 --> 00:04:29,305
Document the expected value type and test representative overrides.

75
00:04:30,680 --> 00:04:32,653
Renaming a token without losing overrides

76
00:04:33,778 --> 00:04:36,079
Renaming a public token is a migration.

77
00:04:36,079 --> 00:04:39,367
During the compatibility period, the new name should win when

78
00:04:39,367 --> 00:04:42,655
supplied, the old name should still work when the new

79
00:04:42,655 --> 00:04:45,942
one is absent, and the library default should remain last.

80
00:04:45,942 --> 00:04:48,244
Put that order in the consuming declaration.

81
00:04:48,244 --> 00:04:50,874
Avoid defining an alias so eagerly that the

82
00:04:50,874 --> 00:04:53,505
old value can no longer reach the fallback.

83
00:04:54,880 --> 00:04:57,750
Renaming a token without losing overrides · 2

84
00:04:58,875 --> 00:05:01,386
Test the four consumer cases: neither name,

85
00:05:01,386 --> 00:05:03,538
old name only, new name only,

86
00:05:03,538 --> 00:05:04,614
and both names.

87
00:05:04,614 --> 00:05:07,842
Include inherited values on a container because testing only

88
00:05:07,842 --> 00:05:10,712
direct host styles can hide the cascade bug.

89
00:05:10,712 --> 00:05:13,582
Publish the replacement, removal version, and migration examples.

90
00:05:13,582 --> 00:05:16,452
A token contract also includes units and meaning.

91
00:05:16,452 --> 00:05:20,397
Renaming a fixed height to a minimum height may improve accessibility

92
00:05:20,397 --> 00:05:23,985
but can change layout, so teams need to review it.

93
00:05:25,360 --> 00:05:28,068
Renaming a token without losing overrides · 3

94
00:05:29,193 --> 00:05:33,256
The eventual removal belongs in a release that follows your compatibility policy.

95
00:05:33,256 --> 00:05:36,642
Forty teams will not all migrate on the same day.

96
00:05:36,642 --> 00:05:39,351
Agree on a support window, supply automated help

97
00:05:39,351 --> 00:05:42,059
where possible, and collect evidence of remaining uses.

98
00:05:42,059 --> 00:05:44,768
Do not remove an override merely because the

99
00:05:44,768 --> 00:05:47,477
library itself no longer uses the old name.

100
00:05:47,477 --> 00:05:50,524
The consumer is the reason the compatibility alias exists.

101
00:05:51,900 --> 00:05:53,815
System themes and application preferences

102
00:05:54,940 --> 00:05:58,914
A theme library should not assume that every application starts

103
00:05:58,914 --> 00:06:02,889
in a browser or stores a preference in local storage.

104
00:06:02,889 --> 00:06:07,261
Server rendering, hydration, embedded applications, and scoped themes have different requirements.

105
00:06:07,261 --> 00:06:10,838
Let CSS handle a system color preference where possible.

106
00:06:10,838 --> 00:06:16,004
An explicit application choice can set a dedicated attribute without replacing unrelated classes.

107
00:06:17,380 --> 00:06:19,846
System themes and application preferences · 2

108
00:06:20,971 --> 00:06:25,261
The light-dark function selects a value according to the used color scheme.

109
00:06:25,261 --> 00:06:28,836
A root that supports light and dark can follow the

110
00:06:28,836 --> 00:06:32,411
system preference, while a scoped explicit scheme can choose one.

111
00:06:32,411 --> 00:06:35,271
For older supported browsers, provide light defaults and

112
00:06:35,271 --> 00:06:38,132
a prefers-color-scheme media query, then explicit theme overrides.

113
00:06:38,132 --> 00:06:41,707
Check the actual browser support policy before selecting the implementation;

114
00:06:41,707 --> 00:06:44,925
the function is a capability, not a universal assumption.

115
00:06:46,300 --> 00:06:48,818
System themes and application preferences · 3

116
00:06:49,943 --> 00:06:53,540
A stored explicit choice needs to be restored before first

117
00:06:53,540 --> 00:06:56,777
paint if the application wants to avoid a flash.

118
00:06:56,777 --> 00:07:00,015
A library can document how the application sets an

119
00:07:00,015 --> 00:07:02,893
attribute during server rendering or initial page setup.

120
00:07:02,893 --> 00:07:07,210
It should not silently choose a storage policy or overwrite body classes.

121
00:07:07,210 --> 00:07:10,807
When JavaScript is needed, inject the document and guard browser-only

122
00:07:10,807 --> 00:07:14,405
APIs; a framework service must still respect the execution environment.

123
00:07:15,780 --> 00:07:17,502
Forced colors and non-color state

124
00:07:18,627 --> 00:07:20,348
Forced-colors mode changes the rules.

125
00:07:20,348 --> 00:07:24,825
The browser can replace author colors with system colors chosen by the user.

126
00:07:24,825 --> 00:07:27,925
A selected chip that differs only by a blue

127
00:07:27,925 --> 00:07:30,680
background may become indistinguishable from an unselected chip.

128
00:07:30,680 --> 00:07:34,812
Communicate selection with an additional cue such as a check mark, border,

129
00:07:34,812 --> 00:07:38,945
or text, and expose the same state through the appropriate ARIA attribute.

130
00:07:40,320 --> 00:07:42,895
Forced colors and non-color state · 2

131
00:07:44,020 --> 00:07:47,698
Use system colors deliberately where a custom indicator needs them.

132
00:07:47,698 --> 00:07:52,111
Highlight and HighlightText form a system-selected pair; GrayText can express disabled text.

133
00:07:52,111 --> 00:07:55,054
Avoid turning off forced color adjustment for a

134
00:07:55,054 --> 00:07:57,996
whole component simply to preserve a brand palette.

135
00:07:57,996 --> 00:08:01,674
A narrowly justified adjustment must still use colors that respect

136
00:08:01,674 --> 00:08:04,985
the user's mode and retain a visible focus indicator.

137
00:08:06,360 --> 00:08:08,999
Forced colors and non-color state · 3

138
00:08:10,124 --> 00:08:13,191
Browser emulation in CI can check that outlines,

139
00:08:13,191 --> 00:08:15,875
selected markers, and disabled states remain rendered.

140
00:08:15,875 --> 00:08:19,709
Automated checks are valuable, but they do not replace testing

141
00:08:19,709 --> 00:08:23,159
on an actual high contrast configuration with assistive technology.

142
00:08:23,159 --> 00:08:26,610
Treat a forced-colors screenshot as evidence of the tested

143
00:08:26,610 --> 00:08:29,677
browser state, not a certification of every platform.

144
00:08:29,677 --> 00:08:32,745
This distinction will matter when we discuss verification.

145
00:08:34,120 --> 00:08:36,153
Text growth, reflow, and target size

146
00:08:37,278 --> 00:08:41,345
Relative font units help controls follow the user's preferred default text size.

147
00:08:41,345 --> 00:08:44,395
A rem is relative to the root font size.

148
00:08:44,395 --> 00:08:47,784
Avoid setting a fixed root size that defeats that preference.

149
00:08:47,784 --> 00:08:52,529
Browser zoom and an increased default font size are different mechanisms, so test both.

150
00:08:52,529 --> 00:08:56,596
A pixel border can be reasonable even when the text is relative;

151
00:08:56,596 --> 00:09:00,325
the goal is usable scaling rather than replacing every unit mechanically.

152
00:09:01,700 --> 00:09:04,387
Text growth, reflow, and target size · 2

153
00:09:05,511 --> 00:09:09,352
Fixed heights, hidden overflow, and non-wrapping labels are a dangerous combination.

154
00:09:09,352 --> 00:09:13,192
Use minimum sizes with padding and allow the control to grow.

155
00:09:13,192 --> 00:09:18,080
Check long translations, two hundred percent text scaling, and the WCAG text-spacing adjustments: line

156
00:09:18,080 --> 00:09:22,618
height of one and a half, paragraph spacing of twice the font size,

157
00:09:22,618 --> 00:09:27,506
letter spacing of point one two em, and word spacing of point one six

158
00:09:27,506 --> 00:09:27,855
em.

159
00:09:27,855 --> 00:09:32,045
No text or essential operation should disappear when those values are applied.

160
00:09:33,420 --> 00:09:36,509
Text growth, reflow, and target size · 3

161
00:09:37,634 --> 00:09:40,554
Separate icon size from hit target size.

162
00:09:40,554 --> 00:09:45,144
A small icon can sit in a much larger native button.

163
00:09:45,144 --> 00:09:48,900
WCAG's minimum target criterion is twenty-four CSS pixels with

164
00:09:48,900 --> 00:09:52,238
defined exceptions; many systems choose larger touch targets.

165
00:09:52,238 --> 00:09:55,994
A dense layout requires checking spacing and exception conditions,

166
00:09:55,994 --> 00:10:00,166
not simply claiming that twenty-four-pixel rows make every target compliant.

167
00:10:00,166 --> 00:10:03,505
Verify the actual clickable rectangle and neighboring targets.

168
00:10:04,880 --> 00:10:07,472
A repeatable review and verification method

169
00:10:08,598 --> 00:10:11,190
Review tokens from the consumer outward.

170
00:10:11,190 --> 00:10:14,647
Identify the supported override, follow its fallback chain,

171
00:10:14,647 --> 00:10:18,104
then examine every visual state that consumes it.

172
00:10:18,104 --> 00:10:21,561
Include light, dark, explicit preferences, inherited scoped overrides,

173
00:10:21,561 --> 00:10:24,586
forced colors, disabled states, focus, enlarged text,

174
00:10:24,586 --> 00:10:25,883
and long labels.

175
00:10:25,883 --> 00:10:29,340
This matrix turns vague reports such as the

176
00:10:29,340 --> 00:10:32,364
theme does not work into reproducible cases.

177
00:10:33,740 --> 00:10:36,767
A repeatable review and verification method · 2

178
00:10:37,892 --> 00:10:41,794
Centralize authoring where designers and engineers can review the same

179
00:10:41,794 --> 00:10:45,696
decisions, and generate platform artifacts rather than manually synchronizing values.

180
00:10:45,696 --> 00:10:50,768
The generated CSS, native platform constants, documentation, and previews should preserve semantic names.

181
00:10:50,768 --> 00:10:56,231
Validate references and types so a missing alias or mismatched unit fails before publication.

182
00:10:56,231 --> 00:10:59,743
A lint rule can prevent new raw values in

183
00:10:59,743 --> 00:11:02,865
component styles while allowing the intentional palette source.

184
00:11:04,240 --> 00:11:07,005
A repeatable review and verification method · 3

185
00:11:08,130 --> 00:11:11,362
As we read the questions, connect every proposed correction

186
00:11:11,362 --> 00:11:14,235
to a failure mechanism and a regression test.

187
00:11:14,235 --> 00:11:18,185
Explain who owns an override and what existing consumers will observe.

188
00:11:18,185 --> 00:11:21,417
That combination demonstrates senior judgment: the design is coherent,

189
00:11:21,417 --> 00:11:25,726
the implementation respects CSS behavior, and the rollout protects teams who depend

190
00:11:25,726 --> 00:11:26,445
on it.

191
00:11:27,820 --> 00:11:30,046
TOK-001 · Scenario

192
00:11:31,171 --> 00:11:35,386
We now review TOK-001: A button that cannot be themed.

193
00:11:35,386 --> 00:11:40,445
Inspect the consumer contract and identify the mechanism behind each reported defect.

194
